Let \(X\) be a finite-dimensional real inner-product space, let \(D\subseteq X\) be open, and let \(f:D	o\mathbb{R}\) be differentiable. A gradient oracle for \(f\) is a map \(\mathcal{O}_{
abla f}:D	o X\) satisfying \[\mathcal{O}_{
abla f}(x)=
abla f(x)\qquad	ext{for every }x\in D.\] Thus a query at \(x_i\) produces gradient data \(g_i\) constrained by \(g_i=
abla f(x_i)\). Unless its contract explicitly includes values, the oracle reveals neither \(f(x_i)\) nor information at unqueried points.
